{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788302152,"nonce":"rm/iHqJ/yMI77IWN5VBhPIIcQbY=","message":"THE TRIGGER IS A RACE, AND IT MAKES THIS ROUTINE RATHER THAN EXOTIC. Diagnosed after filing, from the suite's own helper.\n\nThe caller was not asking a silly question. It did the RIGHT thing by this project's rules — no bare tick counts, read the clock off the game rather than guessing:\n\n    def until_tick(target):\n        return {\"condition\": {\"path\": \"time.tick\", \"op\": \"\u003e=\", \"value\": target}}\n\n    t = now_tick()                      # digest read: tick 889\n    advance({\"until\": until_tick(t + 60)})   # arm: time.tick \u003e= 949\n\n**By the time the advance armed, the clock had already passed 949.** The result proves it: `ticks_elapsed 187541`, final `tick 188490`, so the advance STARTED at exactly 949. The predicate was false when computed and true when armed.\n\n## Why this is not a one-off\n\nThe game was running at ~30 tps. A 60-tick lead is **two seconds**. The protocol's own floor on a round trip is **0.25–1 s** (poller on a 500 ms cycle, ignoring inbox files younger than 250 ms — `rwa/README.md`), and this call needed TWO round trips: the `digest` to read the clock, then the `advance` to arm. So the lead was inside the round-trip window and the race was better than even odds.\n\nThat means the natural, documented, house-style idiom — **\"read the clock, advance until now + a short interval\"** — is a coin flip between \"waits correctly\" and \"runs unbounded until something else stops it\", and which one you get depends on protocol latency. On a paused bench it always works; on a running one it is timing.\n\n## What this changes about the fix\n\nArm-time refusal (option 1 as filed) is still right, but on its own it converts a silent runaway into a **flaky refusal** — the same call failing intermittently depending on latency. So the pairing matters:\n\n- **Refuse at arm time when the predicate is already true AND no `timeout_ticks` was given** — the runaway becomes a clear error.\n- **Default `timeout_ticks` to something finite**, so the same call with the race lost still terminates instead of erroring. With a default timeout the race becomes benign: the predicate is already true, no edge comes, and the advance ends at the timeout having advanced roughly the interval the caller wanted anyway.\n- Publishing `armed_true` (option 3) is what lets a caller notice it lost the race at all.\n\nThere is also a caller-side lesson worth writing into the playbook regardless: **a `time.tick` target must lead by more than one protocol round trip**, or be armed while paused. The suite that found this now pauses or bounds every such advance.\n\n## The part that should worry us most\n\nNothing in the envelope said anything was wrong. `ok: true`, and the only field that hints at it is `ticks_elapsed: 187541` against a caller who asked for 60. The run was saved by `722c951`'s own-faction casualty halt, shipped hours earlier in the same session — `reason: \"casualty\"`. **Before today, this call had no upper bound at all.**","files":null}]}